> taylor sin(x) x at 0 order 7
Maclaurin (Taylor at 0) series of sin(x) at x = 0, through order 7:
  sin(x) = x - x^3/6 + x^5/120 - x^7/5040 + O(x^8)
  as x -> 0; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(0)/k!: 0 1 0 -1/6 0 1/120 0 -1/5040
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 2.76e-15 at x = 0 ± 1/10, 5.38e-18 at ± 1/20, 1.02e-20 at ± 1/40 (at rounding level)
> taylor log(x) x at 1 order 4
Taylor series of log(x) at x = 1, through order 4:
  log(x) = x - 1 - (x - 1)^2/2 + (x - 1)^3/3 - (x - 1)^4/4 + O((x - 1)^5)
  as x -> 1; coefficients c_k = f^(k)(1)/k!: 0 1 -1/2 1/3 -1/4
  check: the coefficients agree with Taylor-jet arithmetic (long double) to 0
  check: |f - P| = 2.18e-06 at x = 1 ± 1/10, 6.52e-08 at ± 1/20, 1.99e-09 at ± 1/40 (shrinks like h^5.0; expected >= 5)
> implicit x^2+y^2=1 for y wrt x
on the curve x^2 + y^2 - 1 = 0, y as a function of x:
  dy/dx = -F_x/F_y = -x/y
  holds where F_y = 2y != 0 (the implicit function theorem: there y is locally a differentiable function of x)
  check: matches the slope of the curve (points solved by bisection and Newton) at 12 points (max relative difference 2e-12)
> x' = 1
> x(0)=0
> dsolve x
x' = 1, x(0) = 0
  (on [0, 10]; 'dsolve x to T' for another end)
  linear with constant coefficients (eigenvalues 0), but no closed form was found for the forcing: numeric only
  numeric (Dormand-Prince 5(4), adaptive, 40 steps, tolerance 1e-10); a 1000x looser solve differs by 0
